    "content": "As of early April 2026, California is experiencing a surge in Human Metapneumovirus (HMPV), particularly in Northern California, characterized by coughing, fever, and congestion. While influenza and COVID-19 activity is declining or low, HMPV and respiratory syncytial virus (RSV) remain active, causing cases of bronchiolitis and croup, especially in children. Top Viruses Circulating in California: Human Metapneumovirus (HMPV): Surging in Northern California (Sacramento, San Francisco, Bay Area), this virus is known to cause severe upper and lower respiratory infections, including croup and bronchiolitis, often mistaken for flu or COVID. RSV (Respiratory Syncytial Virus): Still circulating, causing infections in young children and high-risk individuals. Seasonal Influenza: Activity is declining statewide but still present. COVID-19: Activity remains at very low levels across California. Key Details: Regional Data: Wastewater tracking shows high levels of HMPV in Sacramento, Davis, Vallejo, and the San Francisco Bay Area. Symptoms: HMPV symptoms include cough, fever, congestion, and sometimes a \"barking\" cough or wheezing. Transmission: HMPV spreads through respiratory droplets (coughing/sneezing) and contaminated surfaces. For the latest local trends, check the CDPH Respiratory Virus Dashboard. CDPH Respiratory Virus Dashboard",
